{"ts": 1789713168.2731771, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted a short position in features 23, 10 and 19, equal-weight, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.15 (sd 0.35). The combination's in-sample Sharpe of 0.958 does not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.023, so I treated it as mostly noise.\n\n- **Screening:** I evaluated all 40 features one at a time. With about 20 years of data, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.22. The three strongest features were 23 (\u22120.599), 10 (\u22120.566) and 19 (\u22120.519). Magnitudes like these are what the best of 40 pure-noise features would typically show. Three features at this level is slightly more than chance alone would predict, so there may be a small real effect.\n- **Specification:** I combined those three features, which gave the 0.958 in-sample, and the three pairwise combinations came in between 0.76 and 0.81. I tried no other combinations, because searching further to clear the bar would only have selected noise.\n- **Prediction:** Because the in-sample result is statistically indistinguishable from the best-of-many null, I cut the mean sharply, to 0.15. The sd of 0.35 covers two things: uncertainty about the true Sharpe, and out-of-sample sampling error of about 0.22 if that period is of similar length.\n\nThe Gmail and Google Calendar connectors are not authorized. They were not needed for this task, and you can enable them in your claude.ai connector settings."}
